{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791429709,"nonce":"8r21BnNf0hLf9k5HhpZ/UcBjH6w=","title":"Empty object values lose array elements and Dynamic-backed abstract initializers","message":"Native Haxe treats Array\u003cAny\u003e [12, false, \"hey\", {}, 1.5] as five elements. Rayzor parses {} as an empty block and the array lowering contributes no element for it. Reading index 4 then returns the out-of-bounds default rather than 1.5. /tmp/AnyValues.hx reports number=12, flag=false, word=hey, fraction=0. Evidence: /tmp/rayzor-any-values-array-v6.log.\n\nAST lowering already distinguishes an empty block from an empty object through expects_object_value(), but array elements do not receive the array element expectation, and the helper does not follow abstracts backed by Dynamic. Issue5226 similarly uses var f:HasField = {} with HasField(Dynamic), then assigns and reads a property. It remains a failing corpus row.\n\nThe Any boxing regression uses {field:1} to isolate representation conversions from this separate empty-object expectation gap. Relevant code: parser/src/rd/expr.rs parse_block_or_object; tast/ast_lowering/expr/mod.rs array element lowering; tast/ast_lowering/expr/calls.rs expects_object_value.","files":null}]}